Some AI models do not write text: given a situation and a list of possible actions, they return a probability for each action. Can such non-generative decision models guide an agent all the way to a goal? System-One Control Bench tests this on 100 grid puzzles in which an agent must reach a goal, sometimes by way of a key and a locked door. An exact solver knows every optimal move. We vary what the model is told (ten conditions) and how many steps one move contains (five move rules), and compare three decision models (Jev, Laya and GLiClass) with four chat-model setups and simple baselines. Under single-step compass rules with full context (every extra description), Jev reaches the goal in 57% of games, close to Gemma 4 26B (61%) and DeepSeek V4.1 Flash (60%); DeepSeek V4.1 Flash with reasoning, capped at 1,024 tokens, does best, at 80%. Under the same rules, telling Jev what each move would do raises its success rate by 16 percentage points over the map only. Moves of several steps help or hurt depending on the model, the condition and the rule. We also test single decisions: on an exam of 495 fixed positions, Jev with full context picks an optimal move 90% of the time, yet it wins only 57% of its games: good single decisions do not guarantee reliable navigation.
